\paragraph{SDF regression loss.} The regression loss trains the SDF head to predict accurate signed distances:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{V}} \left(\hat{s}_{i,j} - s_{i,j}\right)^2,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{V}$ is the set of valid sample-position pairs with available ground-truth labels, $\hat{s}_{i,j}$ is the predicted signed distance, and $s_{i,j}$ is the ground truth. Accurate SDF predictions are prerequisite for the collision penalty to produce meaningful gradients.

\paragraph{Collision penalty.} The collision loss penalizes predictions below the safety margin $\delta$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{V}} w(t_i) \cdot \mathrm{ReLU}\!\left(\delta - \hat{s}_{i,j}\right),
\end{equation}
where $\delta = 0.02\,\mathrm{m}$ (2\,cm safety margin) and $t_i$ is the flow-matching noise level for sample $i$. The hinge structure ensures zero penalty when $\hat{s}_{i,j} > \delta$ and linear penalty proportional to violation depth otherwise. The noise-aware weight $w(t_i)$ restricts this shaping signal to the low-noise regime where the model's features encode meaningful spatial information.

\paragraph{Total training objective.} The complete loss combines the flow-matching objective with both auxiliary terms:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:total_loss}
\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FM}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}} + \lambda_{\mathrm{col}} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}},
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} = 0.1$ and $\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0.01$. The small collision weight reflects its role as a shaping signal: the flow-matching loss remains the primary objective, and the collision penalty steers the velocity field toward safer regions without dominating optimization. Despite the small $\lambda_{\mathrm{col}}$, the directional gradient signal accumulates across training steps to measurably shift the velocity field (Section~\ref{sec:main_results}).


